  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Mostly dry and comfortable weather is expected for \nthe rest of the weekend. The exception will be near the coast of New \nJersey into southern Delaware where some showers are possible\ntoday. \n\nA drier and cooler airmass has arrived across the northern Middle \nAtlantic region as high pressure to the northwest begins influencing \nour weather to a greater degree. Low pressure aloft across the the \nNorthern Atlantic continues to bring Northwest flow aloft over our \narea. A couple embedded short waves in this flow will pass across \nNew England and our coastal waters into the day today. This may\nresult in some scattered showers near the New Jersey coast \nthis morning with this activity then drifting south into \nsouthern Delaware by the afternoon. Pops are generally in the \n20% to 35% range, so no significant impacts are expected. \nOtherwise expect a mix of sun and clouds for today with a \nnortheast breeze around 5 to 10 mph. Highs will be in the 70s \nalong the coast and over our northern zones with some low 80s \nover much of Delmarva through the Greater Philadelphia region. \nSimilar conditions will follow for Labor Day with sunshine and \nhighs close to if not a bit warmer than Sunday's highs. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with this \nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the southern Jersey Shore, \nDelaware Beaches, and Delaware Bay communities. A Coastal Flood \nAdvisory remains in effect from 3 PM to 9 PM.\n\nFlow will continue to turn more onshore today. This will result in \nwater piling up over consecutive tide cycles, leading to minor tidal \nflooding across portions of our area this afternoon.\nThe current forecast has minor tidal flooding from Atlantic City on \nsouth along the Atlantic Ocean coastline and for areas within \nDelaware Bay. A Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect for \nAtlantic, Cape May, and Cumberland County in NJ and Kent and Sussex \nCounty in Delaware from 3 PM this afternoon until 9 PM this evening\n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and Chesapeake \nBay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor or no flooding \nat all. Tide levels should decrease getting into next week as flow \nturns more offshore.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will pass through on Thursday bringing \nthe potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nMid-level ridging will be in place across central and northeastern \nportions of the US through Tuesday, with high pressure also in \ncontrol at the surface. The result will be tranquil conditions. \nStarting on Wednesday, ridging aloft is expected to begin to break \ndown as an upper-low tracks eastward from portions of Ontario into \nportions of Quebec. Current guidance has height falls beginning \nacross the area on Wednesday, with a strong shortwave pivoting \nthrough the region on Thursday. At the surface, high pressure will \nshift offshore Wednesday, with a cold front moving through sometime \non Thursday.\n\nWednesday into Thursday, as the high shifts offshore, warm and moist \nsoutherly flow will resume across the area, with highs warming into \nthe mid-upper 80s for many, and dewpoints rising into the 60s.\n\nIt is not entirely out of the question that as the ridge begins to \nbreak down some shower and storm chances could ultimately evolve \nacross portions of the area Wednesday night. We currently feature \nPoPs generally in the 20-30% range for this time period mainly for \nnorthwestern portions of the area. Slightly better shower and storm \nchances are likely to exist across the area on Thursday when the \nactual cold front moves through the region. At this point, PoPs \nthursday are also generally in the 20-30% range, but would not be \nsurprised if they trend up some in the coming days. Currently, the \nthreat for severe storms or flash flooding concerns appears low.\n\nIn the wake of the cold front, dry conditions look to return for \nFriday and Saturday, along with cooler temperatures. High \ntemperatures look to be in the 70s to low 80s with lows in the mid \n50s to mid 60s.",
